WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:04.580
Not every Hollywood release can be a home
run, but you should at least be able to expect

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:07.140
some decent entertainment when you go to the
theater.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:11.670
Every so often, however, even great filmmakers
will churn out a pretty terrible movie.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:16.530
In 2018, these are the films that perked up
our ears, and then sent us home with our hearts

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:20.380
unfulfilled, our wallets empty, and our foreheads
sore from all the facepalming.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.230
(when you find out they made 9 Madea movies
but there's still no sequel to Dredd)

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:25.630
These are the worst movies of the year so
far.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:27.689
And they sure aren't pretty.

00:00:27.689 --> 00:00:29.529
Insidious: The Last Key

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:34.630
Fans of the Insidious series started getting
goosebumps of anticipation when a fourth installment

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:36.590
was announced in 2016.

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:39.330
But it wasn't long before we saw signs of
trouble.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:44.720
Insidious: The Last Key's release date was
pushed back from October 2017 to January of

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:45.720
2018.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:51.360
And as you probably know, January isn't exactly
prime territory for horror releases.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.300
So when the film finally hit theaters, the
reason for the shuffle became extremely apparent:

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.100
it isn't very good.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:03.210
Critics hammered Insidious: The Last Key for
its confused tone and for playing like a mishmash

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:06.020
of well-worn horror tropes.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.260
(Darth Maul returns)

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:15.260
But diminishing creative returns don't always
translate to diminishing box office returns.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:20.220
The film's worldwide gross is actually the
best of the entire series, meaning that Insidious

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.330
5 is all but inevitable.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:26.510
Here's hoping the next installment finds a
way to deliver some bona fide scares while

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.150
moving all those tickets.

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:32.420
(Yup, that's the landlord from Kingpin)

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:33.950
Proud Mary

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:38.890
On the surface, Proud Mary seemed like a sure
thing: it's the story of a contract killer,

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:43.820
played by the immensely talented Taraji P.
Henson, who finds herself responsible for

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:47.410
the welfare of a young boy after a job goes
sideways.

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:52.890
The exciting trailers promised a great performance
from Henson, and she delivered, but unfortunately,

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:57.930
she did so in a film that felt like it was
slapped together out of spare parts.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:03.270
The $14 million flick did dismal business
worldwide, making it a certified flop.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.840
The 15:17 to Paris

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.939
Clint Eastwood has a pretty good track record
as a director, and an even better one as a

00:02:09.939 --> 00:02:11.930
director of historical dramas.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:17.769
So, expectations were high for The 15:17 to
Paris, the true story of a trio of American

00:02:17.769 --> 00:02:21.560
soldiers who found themselves in the middle
of an attempted terrorist attack on a train

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.099
while traveling in Europe.

00:02:23.099 --> 00:02:26.989
The actual story is a harrowing account, and
Eastwood even even recruited the soldiers

00:02:26.989 --> 00:02:29.260
to play themselves in the movie.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:34.159
Unfortunately, it turns out that one spectacular
act of heroism doesn't necessarily make for

00:02:34.159 --> 00:02:38.629
an entertaining film...and real-life heroes
don't always make great actors.

00:02:38.629 --> 00:02:42.380
Even the stars themselves had different ideas
on who should have been in the film.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:46.439
"You must have had ideas in your head of…
who did you want to play you?

00:02:46.439 --> 00:02:47.580
"Zac Efron."

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:48.730
(crowd laughs)

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:52.450
One of the other problems critics had was
that the portrayal of the actual incident

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:57.829
was fairly short, with nearly two-thirds of
the film's run time devoted to exposition.

00:02:57.829 --> 00:03:02.209
While the main action sequence was praised,
everything surrounding it was a disappointment,

00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:08.870
and The 15:17 to Paris arrived at the station
to be greeted by generally indifferent audiences.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:10.700
Midnight Sun

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:15.239
Music video director turned filmmaker Scott
Speer scored a minor hit with his first big-screen

00:03:15.239 --> 00:03:20.610
directorial effort, Step Up: Revolution, a
solid if inessential addition to the Step

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:21.700
Up series.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:28.129
For his next trick, Speer mounted an adaptation
of the 2006 Japanese feature Midnight Sun,

00:03:28.129 --> 00:03:33.159
starring former Disney star Bella Thorne alongside
none other than Patrick Schwarzenegger.

00:03:33.159 --> 00:03:38.450
It's the story of a teenage girl with a rare
and life-threatening genetic sensitivity to

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:42.959
sunlight, who is finally asked out by her
secret crush, but instead of tugging at the

00:03:42.959 --> 00:03:48.060
audience's heartstrings, the film left critics
and audiences alike feeling cold with its

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:51.439
gimmicky portrayal of an actual, terminal
condition.

00:03:51.439 --> 00:03:57.040
The film opened to a dismal $4 million opening
weekend, while critics far and wide advised

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.419
filmgoers to save their money.

00:03:59.419 --> 00:04:00.419
Forever My Girl

00:04:00.419 --> 00:04:05.700
Writer/director Bethany Ashton Wolf helmed
her second big-screen effort with Forever

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:10.980
My Girl, the story of a country music star
who returns home for the funeral of a friend,

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.769
only to face the lost love he left behind.

00:04:13.769 --> 00:04:17.199
If that description strikes as you as the
type of thing that Nicholas Sparks might have

00:04:17.199 --> 00:04:21.959
come up with, you're certainly not alone,
and that's part of the problem, critics couldn't

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:26.360
help but note that Forever failed even to
clear the bar typically set by Sparks' formulaic

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:27.729
film stories.

00:04:27.729 --> 00:04:31.570
The film seemed to bring out the worst in
practically every critic who viewed it, with

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:35.160
most reviewers feeling that it barely lived
up to the standards of a Lifetime movie.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:39.289
"I didn't find out that I was pregnant until
a couple weeks after what was supposed to

00:04:39.289 --> 00:04:40.510
be our wedding day."

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:46.169
The picture managed only a $16 million haul
during its theatrical run, further indication

00:04:46.169 --> 00:04:50.600
that 2018 is decidedly not shaping up to be
a great year for romantic dramas.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:57.140
Sherlock Gnomes

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:02.889
The 2011 animated romp Gnomeo and Juliet was
a surprise hit, raking in nearly $200 million

00:05:02.889 --> 00:05:05.009
at the worldwide box office.

00:05:05.009 --> 00:05:10.539
The story is basically retelling of Shakespeare's
famous starcrossed tragedy but with… well,

00:05:10.539 --> 00:05:11.669
garden gnomes.

00:05:11.669 --> 00:05:13.050
And also slightly less suicide.

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:14.250
"Are you crazy?

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:18.070
If dad finds you here he'll bury you under
the patio."

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:22.169
The film earned mixed reviews, but its box
office returns were more than enough to guarantee

00:05:22.169 --> 00:05:23.590
a sequel.

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:29.330
Sherlock Gnomes spent six years in development,
but was finally released to audiences in 2018.

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:34.630
Unfortunately, all of those years weren't
spent crafting a, y'know, watchable movie.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:40.259
Despite the presence of Johnny Depp in the
title role and Chiwetel Ejiofor as Watson,

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:42.629
critics and audiences were bored to tears
by this outing.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:44.780
"We'll need a ship."

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:46.159
"No ship, Sherlock."

00:05:46.159 --> 00:05:51.200
Many noted that, like the original, the film
seems to exist as a delivery service for the

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.830
music of executive producer Elton John.

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:57.860
The film's box office struggles may mean that
we'll be spared another trip to Gnome world

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:59.310
in the future, however.

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:01.520
So at least there's that.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:02.819
Death Wish

00:06:02.819 --> 00:06:07.670
There may not be a major Hollywood film that
has ever arrived with worse timing than Death

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:08.670
Wish.

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:13.620
The 2018 remake features Bruce Willis as Paul
Kersey, an everyday Joe whose daughter and

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:15.759
wife are assaulted by thugs.

00:06:15.759 --> 00:06:19.370
When the police don't do anything, he takes
matters into his own hands.

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:22.340
"So there's nothing I can do, is that what
you're saying?"

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:23.550
"You can have faith."

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:27.819
Critics certainly took note of the film's
incredibly poor timing given that the Stoneman

00:06:27.819 --> 00:06:31.000
Douglas High School shooting had occurred
just a few weeks earlier.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:35.680
But aside from that, Death Wish just doesn't
really work as a movie anyway.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:38.840
Viewed as a pure action film, it's surprisingly
sluggish.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:44.069
The Charles Bronson original famously spawned
four sequels of diminishing returns and escalating

00:06:44.069 --> 00:06:45.280
insanity.

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:48.320
"What are those?"

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:50.120
"Teeth!"

00:06:50.129 --> 00:06:55.490
With this version failing to strike box office
gold, history probably won't repeat itself.

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:57.210
The Cloverfield Paradox

00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:02.979
During the Super Bowl in February 2018, a
surprise trailer dropped for The Cloverfield

00:07:02.979 --> 00:07:08.469
Paradox, the third installment in J.J. Abrams'
loosely connected sci-fi/horror series.

00:07:08.469 --> 00:07:12.729
The flick was released to Netflix directly
after the game, and millions of eager fans

00:07:12.729 --> 00:07:15.630
tuned in… to be supremely disappointed.

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:20.340
Despite the presence of an impressive cast,
Paradox was a disappointing dud, with much

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:24.620
of the criticism heaped on its undercooked
characters and exposition-heavy script.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:29.580
The film was originally intended for a theatrical
release, and Paramount COO Andrew Gumpert

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:33.790
explained why the decision was made to distribute
it on Netflix, saying:

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:38.590
"We all decided that there were things about
it that made us have a pause about its commercial

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:40.689
playability in the traditional matter."

00:07:40.689 --> 00:07:45.349
If you think that seems like a delicately
worded way of saying that the film was the

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:48.800
opposite of good, well...you may be onto something.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:57.740
"That's my f---ing arm."

00:07:58.940 --> 00:07:59.939
Winchester

00:07:59.939 --> 00:08:03.939
On paper, Winchester sounded like the kind
of horror slam-dunk that comes along once

00:08:03.939 --> 00:08:10.599
every decade or so: a supernatural fright-fest
based on the spooky true story of Sarah Winchester,

00:08:10.599 --> 00:08:13.530
who spent years building the mother of all
haunted houses.

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:15.199
"Why all the noise?

00:08:15.199 --> 00:08:16.870
Why all this construction?"

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:19.330
"They want me to build, doctor.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:20.689
The spirits."

00:08:20.689 --> 00:08:25.699
Writing/directing duo the Spierig Brothers
had recently scored a nifty sci-fi hit with

00:08:25.699 --> 00:08:30.939
2015's Predestination, and with Helen Mirren
in the lead role of Winchester, the stars

00:08:30.939 --> 00:08:32.360
were all aligned.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:35.190
So how did it go so terribly wrong?

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:39.390
Critics agreed that it represented a hugely
missed opportunity that led to an utterly

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:40.900
forgettable film.

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:45.320
The Spierigs were taken to task for choosing
jump scares over atmosphere.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:50.650
In the end, Winchester became perhaps the
most disappointing film of the year so far.

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:52.900
Fifty Shades Freed

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:56.260
Nobody thought the third installment in the
Fifty Shades series would become a critical

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:59.750
hit… and, well, nobody was disappointed.

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:06.480
Despite raking in almost $370 million worldwide,
Fifty Shades Freed was torn apart by critics.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:11.410
The few positive reviews tended to highlight
its merits as unintentional satire rather

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:14.180
than any redeeming qualities it might have.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:18.870
But there is one cinematic achievement that
Fifty Shades Freed deserves credit for: putting

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:21.470
an end to the Fifty Shades series.

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:23.250
A Wrinkle in Time

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:28.000
Expectations were sky high for director Ava
DuVernay's adaptation of the classic young

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.290
adult novel A Wrinkle in Time.

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:36.270
With an amazing cast that included Storm Reid,
Reese Witherspoon and Oprah Winfrey, the film

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:40.840
looked like it'd be another winner for Disney,
but unfortunately, it fell surprisingly flat

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:44.380
with critics, and posted flat box office receipts
to match.

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:49.010
Not surprisingly, the negative reviews mostly
focused on the disappointment fans of the

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:54.330
novel felt, as the adaptation ultimately failed
to match the source material's tone.

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:59.180
A Wrinkle in Time is a novel that most fans
have always considered un-adaptable, and it

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:00.680
looks like they were right.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:04.660
One review described it as a film that "does
not stop feeling generic."

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:07.980
Another hinted that the film might have been
improved with better editing.

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:12.350
Apparently, it wasn't just one thing that
went wrong, but if you put them all together,

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:16.560
A Wrinkle in Time is nothing more than a bright,
colorful dud.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:17.560
The Outsider

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:22.210
The last five years or so have been full of
ups and downs for Jared Leto.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:27.040
The actor went from an Oscar win for his role
in Dallas Buyers Club to a target for critical

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:31.040
disdain after his portrayal of the Joker in
Suicide Squad.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:35.950
And Leto's career rollercoaster continues
with the Netflix original film The Outsider.

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:41.340
In this movie, Leto stars as a former American
soldier who joins the Yakuza in post-World

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:47.020
War II Japan, a character that has been called
"among the least engaging cinematic protagonists

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:48.190
in recent memory."

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:53.890
The film itself was mercilessly bashed as
a "clueless misfire" and "another big swing-and-a-miss"

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:54.940
for Leto.

00:10:54.940 --> 00:10:56.120
Ouch.

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:57.580
Acrimony

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:01.410
It's safe to say that Taraji P. Henson is
having a rough year.

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:05.550
In addition to the colossal disappointment
that was Proud Mary, she takes the lead role

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:08.670
in Acrimony, the latest film from Tyler Perry.

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:13.360
Henson portrays a wife whose cheating husband
pushes her to the brink of insanity, but her

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:17.550
presence simply wasn't enough to save this
limp, derivative thriller.

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:22.040
Reviews compared the movie to a slower, less
interesting version of Fatal Attraction.

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:26.220
Despite Perry's name virtually guaranteeing
a profit, Acrimony failed to even match the

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:30.290
box office performance of his latest Madea
movie, which is saying a lot.

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:32.280
"Hush, I'm trying to be incogarico."

00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:33.460
"Incogarico?

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:37.540
We're not trying to be incogarico this weekend."

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:38.730
Thanks for watching!

00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:41.170
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:43.320
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

